From 9ea2ab01836c989372fcf037685e725f98ae0a9b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 17:52:58 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] v2.8.5a: Flow Explorer, Let's Encrypt, LAN anti-lockout, and
 two quiet ones

Five fixes, three of them from the field and two found by tooling written for
this rung. Test spin: v2.8.5 stays free for the release build so a test image
and a shipped one can never be confused in a log.

THE FLOW EXPLORER HAS BEEN EMPTY SINCE v2.8.3. Its poll answered 200 and the
page said "No active flows in the accelerator cache" forever. v2.8.3 put a CSRF
gate on reaper_conn.cgi - correctly, it is an uncapped conntrack read a
cross-site GET could drive - but Reaper_Conn.asp's poll was never taught to send
http_id, so every request was refused. do_reaper_conn_cgi takes no action
parameter, so unlike the gk/dev/export CGIs it has no read-only branch to exempt:
every request needs the token. The page now sends it, and a refusal renders the
error instead of falling through to the ordinary empty state - a page that cannot
read the accelerator must not look like an idle one. That ambiguity is what made
this cost a field report rather than a glance.

LET'S ENCRYPT ANNOUNCED A RENEWAL THAT HAD NOT HAPPENED AND A LOGOUT THAT NEVER
CAME. Reported on v2.7.8 and again on v2.8.3, the second time with a certificate
that had renewed days earlier and was still in date. Two independent causes.
  le_restart_httpd is set by libletsencrypt.so on every start_letsencrypt(), and
  start_services() runs that at EVERY boot - not only on a renewal. httpd unsets
  the flag as it starts, so anything seen afterwards was set by the blob during
  that boot. The alert therefore fired on the first login after a flash.
  And nothing in the tree ever logged the session out. The complete consumer set
  for that flag was: httpd unsetting it at startup, state.js alerting on it, and
  state.js clearing it. The promise was unbacked.
  It matters because httpd installs a new certificate only while login_ip is
  empty (reload_cert == 2), so the logout was not cosmetic - it was the step that
  completes the swap. Now: alert only when httpds_reload_cert says a swap is
  really pending, clear the flag quietly when it is not, and perform the logout.
  httpds_reload_cert is appended LAST in ajax_status.xml because state.js indexes
  <wan> elements positionally.
  Third half of the same problem: only the DDNS page's Apply ran the prepare_cert
  rc verb, so a background inadyn-driven renewal left the new certificate on disk
  and httpd kept serving the old one. ddns_updated() now arms the same reload.
  No dict change - with the gate and the logout, the shipped wording is true.

THE LAN ANTI-LOCKOUT RULE WAS WRONG ON ANY LAN THAT IS NOT /24, /16 OR /8.
reaper_fw derived LANNET by pattern-matching the netmask, and everything else
fell to a /24 default. On a /23 or /22 LAN that covers a quarter or a half of the
network, and every host outside it got NO anti-lockout RETURN - the "half my LAN
is down" class. rwarden hit this first and was fixed in v2.8.3; reaper_fw kept
the defective table. This is that same proven arithmetic block.
  Verified without a router and without trusting the escaping: the emitted C
  string was extracted, compiled on the host, and the shell it really produces
  run against a mask table. 10/10 correct - /24, /25 both halves, /23, /22, /16,
  /8, /32 and bogus input - against 6 of 9 wrong before. The %% here is correct
  and load-bearing: ${LANIP%%.*} is the intended longest-match strip, and the
  regression test fails if a single-% form ever reappears.

GATEKEEPER COULD REPORT A DEFERRAL THAT NEVER HAPPENED. /tmp/gk/apply.sh ended on
`fc flush`, and a script's exit status is its last command's, which
start_gk()/gk_refresh() read as the cold-boot LAN deferral. Identical in shape to
the rwarden defect fixed in v2.8.3. It now ends `exit 0`; every rule command
above that is still checked on its own. Whether this fires in practice depends on
what `fc flush` returns on metal, which is one command to settle.

CLIENTS PINNED TO ADGUARD WERE SILENTLY UNFILTERED. Removing AdGuard in v2.7.6
blanked entries 19 and 20 of server_table[]. Nothing broke - get_dns_filter()
returns a count and every caller guards on it, so a blank entry emits no DNAT
rule and DNS keeps working, exactly as the discontinued Norton entries 2-4 have
upstream for years. But a client the user had set to AdGuard is now unfiltered
while they believe ads are blocked, and the dropdown shows an out-of-range value.
  A one-shot idempotent migration rewrites the stored mode to Unfiltered - what
  the box is already doing - and logs each affected client so a replacement can
  be chosen. Deliberately NOT substituted with another provider: silently
  redirecting someone's DNS to a third party we picked would be the bigger
  surprise. SDN profiles are reported, not rewritten; that record is positionally
  dense and a mis-placed field would corrupt a whole profile.
  The rewrite loop was compiled on the host and run over nine cases: 190 is not
  mistaken for 19, a malformed 4-field record is left alone, truncation refuses
  to write rather than dropping clients' settings, and it is a true no-op for
  anyone who never used AdGuard.

TOOLING, none of which ships in the image. A pre-build gate now exists at
/home/reaper/reaper_tests (rule-29 quote safety, CSRF caller coherence, dict
lockstep, and a JS parse of every Reaper page in all 25 languages - ~7s, and it
is what caught state.js being outside the checked set). reaper_verify gained
check 19, csrf-callers, self-tested against history: it flags the v2.7.7 QoS
defect and this rung's Conn defect, and is clean before either existed. Two bugs
in that tooling were found and fixed before it could do harm - it crashed on any
staged www because of the dangling user*.asp symlinks, which would have blocked
every ship, and it had drifted from the lean-repo engine.

Verified across all six model trees: the seven shared files are byte-identical
and the suite is green on each.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
